{"pdf_page": 362, "book_page": 353, "items": [{"label": "text", "text": "(It doesn't matter what variations of colours or dots and crosses are used. The F atoms could also be drawn in different positions.)", "bbox": [79.5, 711.1, 288.4, 676.9]}, {"label": "picture", "text": "", "bbox": [46.7, 673.8, 142.0, 582.7]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "b The B atom does not have 8 electrons in its outer shell.", "bbox": [67.9, 578.5, 300.8, 568.1]}, {"label": "section_header", "text": "CHAPTER 9", "bbox": [52.8, 552.2, 109.0, 538.4]}, {"label": "picture", "text": "", "bbox": [45.1, 531.7, 258.1, 336.1]}, {"label": "caption", "text": "lattice of metal ions", "bbox": [90.1, 339.1, 162.4, 329.8]}, {"label": "text", "text": "(The diagrams should show at least 9 ions but can be of any size beyond that. Electrons could be shown as e-.)", "bbox": [78.8, 326.0, 295.6, 291.4]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "Mg 2, 8, 2. The two outer electrons will be lost from each Mg atom to form the sea of delocalised electrons. There will be a regular arrangement of Mg²+ ions.", "bbox": [78.1, 286.6, 299.0, 253.1]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "Na will form Na* ions, Mg will form Mg2+ ions and Al will form Al³+ ions. There will be stronger electrostatic attraction between the metal ions and the delocalised electrons when the charge on the ion is higher. Al³+ has the strongest attraction between the metals ions and the delocalised electrons, therefore most energy has to be supplied to overcome the forces of attraction.", "bbox": [78.1, 251.7, 299.0, 174.3]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "Delocalised electrons are free to move.", "bbox": [78.8, 172.6, 239.1, 161.6]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "The layers of metal ions are able to slide over each other without changing the bonding.", "bbox": [77.8, 159.1, 286.0, 136.4]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "giant covalent", "bbox": [78.5, 131.5, 139.8, 120.1]}, {"label": "list_item", "text": "b molecular", "bbox": [67.5, 118.4, 122.3, 108.0